NNAAMMEE
     cchhoowwnn — change file owner and group

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     cchhoowwnn [--hh] [--RR [--HH | --LL | --PP]] _o_w_n_e_r[:_g_r_o_u_p] _f_i_l_e _._._.
     cchhoowwnn [--hh] [--RR [--HH | --LL | --PP]] :_g_r_o_u_p _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     cchhoowwnn sets the user ID and/or the group ID of the specified files.

     The options are as follows:

     --HH      If the --RR option is specified, symbolic links on the command line
             are followed.  Symbolic links encountered in the tree traversal
             are not followed.

     --hh      Treat symbolic links like other files: modify links instead of
             following them.  The --hh and --RR options are mutually exclusive.

     --LL      If the --RR option is specified, all symbolic links are followed.

     --PP      If the --RR option is specified, no symbolic links are followed.

     --RR      Recurse.  Where _f_i_l_e is a directory, change the user ID and/or
             the group ID of the directory and all the files and directories
             in the file hierarchy below it.

     The --HH, --LL, and --PP options are ignored unless the --RR option is specified;
     if none of them are given, the default is to not follow symbolic links.
     In addition, these options override each other and the command's actions
     are determined by the last one specified.

     The _o_w_n_e_r and _g_r_o_u_p operands are both optional; however, one must be
     specified.  If the _g_r_o_u_p operand is specified, it must be preceded by a
     colon (‘:’) character.

     The _o_w_n_e_r may be either a numeric user ID or a user name.  If a user name
     is also a numeric user ID, the operand is used as a user name.  The _g_r_o_u_p
     may be either a numeric group ID or a group name.  If a group name is
     also a numeric group ID, the operand is used as a group name.

     By default, cchhoowwnn clears the set-user-ID and set-group-ID bits on the
     file to prevent accidental or mischievous creation of set-user-ID and
     set-group-ID programs.

     Only the superuser is permitted to change the owner of a file.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The cchhoowwnn utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

